WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:03.980
Here is authorize.

2
00:00:03.980 --> 00:00:05.544
Two lines do the work.

3
00:00:05.544 --> 00:00:08.245
The other twelve time it and retry it.

4
00:00:08.467 --> 00:00:12.271
The same plumbing is copied into capture, refund and void.

5
00:00:12.500 --> 00:00:16.197
An interceptor is that plumbing, written once, for any method.

6
00:00:17.400 --> 00:00:22.693
It gets the call as one object: the method's name, its arguments, and proceed().

7
00:00:22.693 --> 00:00:24.347
proceed() means carry on.

8
00:00:25.867 --> 00:00:28.287
Line up several and you have a chain.

9
00:00:28.287 --> 00:00:30.118
proceed() runs the next one.

10
00:00:30.118 --> 00:00:32.735
After the last, it runs the real method.

11
00:00:33.267 --> 00:00:35.375
Now the issuer is unavailable.

12
00:00:35.375 --> 00:00:36.851
The real call throws.

13
00:00:36.851 --> 00:00:39.943
Retry catches it, and calls proceed() again.

14
00:00:40.567 --> 00:00:41.145
Predict.

15
00:00:41.145 --> 00:00:43.673
What does the timer outside record?

16
00:00:45.400 --> 00:00:47.697
One call, as long as both attempts.

17
00:00:47.697 --> 00:00:51.963
That's what the caller waited for, so @Timed goes outside @Retry.

18
00:00:52.767 --> 00:00:55.365
Who puts the chain in front of a component?

19
00:00:55.365 --> 00:00:57.239
The container, through a proxy.

20
00:00:57.467 --> 00:01:01.927
With an interface, the JDK makes a proxy class that implements it.

21
00:01:01.927 --> 00:01:06.589
One handler runs the chain, then calls the target, a separate object.

22
00:01:06.800 --> 00:01:12.394
With no interface, Byte Buddy writes a subclass that runs the chain, then calls super.

23
00:01:12.394 --> 00:01:18.184
So a final class can't be intercepted, and Kotlin classes are final unless you open them.

24
00:01:18.400 --> 00:01:20.924
The container refuses them at start-up.

25
00:01:21.733 --> 00:01:23.638
Last: which call to retry.

26
00:01:23.638 --> 00:01:26.568
authorizeAndCapture is a default method.

27
00:01:26.568 --> 00:01:28.985
It calls authorize, then capture.

28
00:01:29.200 --> 00:01:30.712
The capture times out.

29
00:01:30.712 --> 00:01:35.933
Retry the whole method, and authorize runs again: a second hold on the card.

30
00:01:36.167 --> 00:01:41.848
With invokeDefault, each inner call has its own chain, so only the capture is retried.

31
00:01:42.067 --> 00:01:48.111
And only with an idempotency key, the same on every attempt, so the issuer can drop the repeat.

32
00:01:48.933 --> 00:01:52.012
Here is the lab's timer, in Java and Kotlin.

33
00:01:54.833 --> 00:01:55.430
The rule.

34
00:01:55.430 --> 00:01:59.472
An interceptor runs around a call, and proceed() hands it on.

35
00:01:59.472 --> 00:02:02.917
Retry only the smallest call that is safe to repeat.

36
00:02:03.133 --> 00:02:04.160
Check yourself.

37
00:02:04.160 --> 00:02:08.748
@Timed sits inside @Retry, and the call works on the third attempt.

38
00:02:08.748 --> 00:02:11.281
How many times does the timer record?

39
00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:14.465
Three.

40
00:02:14.465 --> 00:02:17.723
Inside the retry, it records each attempt.

41
00:02:17.723 --> 00:02:19.972
Outside, it would record one.
